ermarkets here in upstate new  york in  the rochester and canandaigua area  and looking on  how we can  promote these sort of higher quality um  variable types with the small fruit  where you have the individual bite which  is ultimately what consumers prefer  so  a lot of that  selection is done in upstate new york  where we get a lot of  lights early blight uh septoria blights  particularly  uh growing the advanced selections in  tunnels helps considerably with that  these tunnels were recently constructed  so the planting is a little bit late  this year we just uh  pruned and trellised the plants it's  early july and muddy here  but these plants now will probably be  ten times the size in about three or  four weeks  but it it represents some  diversity  of  genotypes and germplasm  that really contribute to making food a  little bit more interesting in  in common being we've also gone in the  same direction  i have now uh mini kidney beans black  kidney beans  purple kidney beans  ones that  you might say are familiar but also  different people are familiar of course  with dark reds so moving to purple or  black  is  not that much of a change  so my main crop  group  is the brassica vegetables  they comprise um  probably  50 or 100 different  market classes because even within  cabbage you can have  savoy and red and flat dutch etc  so traditionally it's had more focus on  cabbage which of course we still work on  developing f1 hybrids of rose cabbage  for testing currently  and also the the other main market  classes of brassica-like broccoli and  cauliflower  more recently over the last decade or so  uh that focus has moved a lot to  the leafy brassicas  uh the kales  and the kale groupings  uh that started probably about 2008 when  i started working on sukuma wiki in  eastern africa which is an  african kale type  and then as the market for kale started  to develop in the u.s for  salad markets in particular  a lot of those efforts started to  get um  sort of fused in  to a common focus of selecting for uh  color and shape in the leafy types  so there's a lot of variation among the  market classes of brassicas  you have brussels sprouts with um  auxiliary buds cabbages essentially a  terminal bud  but  in screen and a lot of the germplasm  we started to focus a lot on  leaf color  uh that included uh the the yellow  golden leaf color that's found in  cabbage collard  uh we started that project in about 2009  2010.  and also started to focus on a lot of  anthocyanins which produced the the  purple colors in  the leaves of the brassicas uh looking  at  brassicas as a source of natural colors  with anthocyanins  so as  consumer demand for  different and more diverse products has  occurred  these different projects have really  started to come together  with the different colors shapes and  work that we're doing in  the different groups  broccoli is the  primary  brassica vegetable  in the u.s  it is also a bit of a one-hit wonder  it's  dominated primarily by the crown cut  market even though you get some  sprouting  types for example  i've been part of the  scri east coast broccoli project for the  last 10 11 years  and that's been a multi-state effort to  try and develop broccoli better suited  to  the climatic conditions on the east  coast  and as part of that effort we've been  developing materials that are more  tolerant for instance  of heat  when it comes to the crown  quality  and that's involved a lot of regional  testing working with  a lot of the big seed companies like  seminars and gender beijing  and right now we have a couple of  uh co-hybrids in advanced trials with  those companies as  varieties that  i would suit not just the east coast but  also  other areas where you have more variable  climate  that effort has been a lot of focus on  developing inbreds cms lines  complementation  but it's also merged with  efforts for  color and diversity that i've had across  the cabbage groups  so broccoli tends to fall in the  the lighter green to darker blue-green  leaf type  and you also see that in the broccoli  crown  so most of the broccoli crowns are  a dark green to a blue-green color  but what we've been trying to do is to  try and  expand out that variation a little bit  to create new and different market  classes of broccoli  the plants that i have here are actually  uh f1 combinations from the greenhouse  so they don't fully show  the trait differences  but this is a hybrid between regular  broccoli and golden broccoli  so it's intermediate in leaf color and  in crown color  if it was later in the season we'd have  the golden broccolis in the field  we also have combinations with kale  material to form uh tuscan broccoli  which has this savoy leaf type in the  broccoli background  and we brought in the anthocyanins for  burgundy broccoli  so  while you have golden broccoli tuscan  broccoli and burgundy broccoli  in these cases they're all intermediate  because they've been crossed to the  regular broccoli  leaf types  but it will give opportunities then to  have more color variation in broccoli  grounds than sprouts and  we've also found through preliminary  nutritional and consumer studies  that you get different  tastes  textures and eating qualities from  the different colored brassicas  so for instance if you have the  the golden  type of colored or kale  they tend to be  milder  more  tender  where if you have the more blue-green  type they tend to be  maybe a little bit more bitter  a little tougher  so  the kale effort is interesting because  it initiated as i mentioned in east  africa probably thirteen or fourteen  years ago  uh sakura wiki or african kale uh sakura  wiki in swahili means to push the week  have enough food for the week it's a  it's a brassica that you can harvest  weekly throughout the season instead of  risking everything on a single crop like  a cabbage or a broccoli  so we'd initially started off with that  project with uh blackrock resistance but  as the u.s kale market developed  we really started to put a big effort in  that direction  kale is a bit of a generic term for a  leafy brassica  primarily there are  the curly types which are the most  common the boracle types  there's the uh the savoid or crinkle  types which are the lacinato or the  tuscan  and there's the flat leaf types which  are the collards kale the forage kale  types  so we also see the jagged leaf types but  that's more associated with the the  russian or siberian kales which are in  the brassica naples group not in the  brassica roller racing group  a large focus over the last few years  has been on  the lacinato type  there's certain benefits to working on  it in the fact it tends to have  more self compatibility for seed  production  and like broccoli it's also a pretty  limited group the cavallo de niro  lacinato dinosaur type it tends to just  be this blue green crinkle type  of ops that are not particularly  vigorous  so among the quality traits we've been  bringing in the leaf color differences  and the  the color from the anthocyanin  so instead of  the blue green type  we can then end up with  leaf types that have the golden leaf or  the green leaf type  that's determined genetically and by  the wax content  on the leaf  but what we're also able to do is  combine  the  leaf type with um the anthocyanins  so we can start producing for example um  golden tuscan kale with a purple vein  or  we can produce  um  green tuscan with a purple vein  or blue-green tuscan with a purple vein  the association among consumers with  kale is typically is a dark leafy green  vegetable  a lot of the mainstream  salad crops tend to be more golden green  if you look at romaine or arugula  and so the familiarity is perhaps more  in the golden green category  so while we have  limited materials to show because it's  early in the season and these are just  out of the greenhouse  what we've been doing is having a focus  on a  a diverse medley  of  tuscan kale types  working on golden tuscan green tuscan  blue green tuscan purple tuscan  and also a golden tuscan with a purple  vein green tuscan with a purple vein  and we can also then put that into a  tuscan collard type and we have a lot in  that group tuscan having the savoy as  opposed to  say a flat leaf collard like this which  would be  a golden colored with a  purple vein  so  having them all in the same market class  background has some  interesting benefits from a sort of  spring mix standpoint because  you can  grow them in the same area under the  same conditions  and you're going to have a similar  product that just varies a lot with  color  but it's also going to vary somewhat  with the  eaten texture  and appearance  the  u.s market for leafy brassicas is  dominated  by the salad market  and there's certain  criteria  that  make a leafy type desirable for the  salad market  a lot of that is determined  by  this texture and structure  within the leaf  when you produce a salad the texture  will  contribute a  number of um  traits of importance  the volume it gives it makes the meal  look larger  and the texture gives it more  structure and  shelf  life stability  but it also helps to develop a crunch uh  to be able to hold the salad dressing  for example food scientists  maybe wouldn't agree in the fact that if  you have a very crinkled or very curly  type  there's more places for pathogens to  hide outer but there's also more places  for the  salad dressing to attach to and hide  after that  so an effort  currently is  focused on  the  kale types primarily within the tuscan  but also within the curly and the flat  leaf  for the mature markets  with an increase in focus on the baby  green markets  the baby green markets  are a group that  are increasing significantly for  multiple reasons  including  technology and venture capital  investment in things like vertical farms  people always also  have a higher demand for pre-mixed  spring green salads  and in that they like a lot of  difference in  color texture  and  shape  and taste among  the crops  so right now we're also trying to take  these groups and find which ones might  be more suited to  the baby green  uh market  uh particularly ones that  add something that's  a little bit more  diverse or  unusual to the mix  the  tuscan kales certainly fall in to that  category  the curly kale's less so because seed  production is more difficult  and the flat leaf less so because the  flat leaf  doesn't have that crunch salad dress  intends to slip off it  and they tend to be um  more for the uh the cooked market than  from the raw  leafy green market  there  certainly  is demand  for uh leaf structure  and shape  changes  so that's something that we have been  increasingly looking at  trying to identify lines that are  vigorous seed producers  so these are what i would call jagged  leaf selections  and you could see  that we can take a  highly jagged leaf  we could take a  moderately jagged leaf or we could take  a flatter  jacket leaf  so there's variations in  the extent  of  how jagged the leaf is  people are more familiar with this in  the the russian kale background um red  russian white russian siberian frills  fizz is a brassica roller racing that's  out there from tozer that has some  jagged leaf look to it  but what we're looking at these for  primarily is the baby green market not  the mature market  with the expansion of  the vertical farms  the expansion of the organic spring  green mixers  and the expansion of brassicas within  spring mixes to start with  uh we're looking at lines that will  contribute  some um  unique looks and types  while also being functional for  the seed production  so  we're looking at these with a number of  companies and  we can take the highly jagged the  moderately jagged  or we could even do uh  a really incredibly jagged type as a  mature  type and  combined in the pigmented leaf type  so  the brassicas are generally a very  interesting group  they have a common and established  association with nutrition  um kale is high in  a number of vitamins vitamin a vitamin k  it's also high in  um some of the carotenoids  like zeaxanthin and lutein that are  important in macular or eye health  so  increasing their presence in spring  green mixes  as  both desirable  and i think  offers huge opportunity just because of  the huge variation that exists within  brassica roller racia as a species  so a lot of what we've already been  doing is focused on  the look and the color and the taste and  the texture  but it's really a type of a fusion  breeden you might say  so taking  taking desirable consumer traits from  one market class  introducing them to  another market class  and by doing that we've been able to  introduce the golden leaf trait to  several market classes of brassicas  and they are now starting to get into  advanced testing and line development  both as releases as ops but also as  releases as f1 cms  hybrids  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu


